From aac026789d179c38df5ae8845395279eae22de1e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Matthias=20M=C3=BCnch?= <claude@djbase.de>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 22:37:20 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] bebbosshd: SFTP/shell bounds fixes and AllowTcpForwarding

Post-authentication hardening found in a full server audit. Affects
bebbosshd only.

- SFTP READ: the read length was only clamped to the remaining file
  size, then read straight into the reply buffer (outdata). A client
  asking for a large read of a large file overflowed it. Clamped to the
  advertised max read (32768-32).
- SFTP WRITE: the write length is client-supplied and was passed to
  Write() without checking it against the packet, reading adjacent buffer
  memory into the file. Rejected when it exceeds the packet.
- setAttrs(): the attribute fields (owner/group lengths, times) were
  parsed without bounds; now bounded by the packet end.
- shell: in the running-forward path the pending input was appended to
  line[CHUNKSIZE] without a cap, unlike the line editor; a flood could
  overflow it. The session is dropped instead.
- SFTP SYMLINK: the target pointer was cast to size_t; use LONG.
- new config AllowTcpForwarding (default yes); "no" refuses direct-tcpip
  (port forwarding) with an administratively-prohibited failure.

Tested on the V4SA over a LAN: echo/exec, and SFTP download and upload
(400 KB) bit-identical via bebboscp. The pre-auth surface was already
hardened (0013) and the public-key auth bypass fixed (0014); this is not
a full fuzz audit.

